        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Tekton tools made in the USA?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Tekton is headquartered in Grand Rapids, Michigan, and their engineering and quality control operations are US-based. However, the tools themselves are manufactured primarily in Taiwan and China. This is common across the industry — many respected brands produce their tools in Asian facilities while maintaining rigorous quality standards through engineering specifications and inspection protocols. The manufacturing origin does not diminish the quality; the engineering standards and material specifications do.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Tekton have a lifetime warranty?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Yes. Every Tekton hand tool carries a lifetime hassle-free guarantee. If a tool fails during normal use, Tekton replaces it free of charge with no receipt required. Their warranty process is widely regarded as one of the fastest and most painless in the industry — replacements typically ship within 24 to 48 hours of a claim, often before the defective tool is returned.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Tekton tools as good as Snap-on?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              In terms of raw material quality and core functionality, Tekton tools deliver approximately 85 to 90 percent of Snap-on's performance at roughly 25 to 35 percent of the price. Snap-on maintains advantages in finish refinement, proprietary jaw geometries, ratchet mechanism smoothness, and the tool truck service model. Whether those advantages justify a three-to-four-times price premium depends on your budget and professional requirements. For most users, Tekton's quality-to-price ratio is significantly better.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are Tekton's best tools?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Tekton's strongest product categories are combination wrenches, impact sockets, torque wrenches, hex key sets, and breaker bars. These tools offer exceptional quality-to-price ratios and compete directly with brands costing two to four times more. Their screwdrivers and pliers are good but face stiffer competition from European specialists like Wera, Wiha, and Knipex in those specific categories.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can you buy Tekton tools?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Tekton sells primarily through their own website (tekton.com) and Amazon. They do not sell through traditional retail stores or tool trucks. The direct-to-consumer model is a key reason their prices are lower than comparable brands — by cutting out retailer and distributor markups, they pass the savings directly to the buyer. Both purchasing channels offer access to the full product lineup and the same lifetime warranty coverage.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Tekton torque wrenches accurate?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Yes. In our testing against a calibrated reference torque tester, Tekton click-type torque wrenches consistently fell within plus or minus four percent accuracy across the middle 80 percent of their operating range. This meets the industry-standard tolerance for click-type torque wrenches and is comparable to units costing three to five times more. Like all click-type torque wrenches, accuracy degrades at the extreme low end of the range, so avoid using them below 20 percent of their maximum rated capacity.
            </p>
